1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,470
Chai Corner is ready to
open its sixth outlet.

2
00:00:03,770 --> 00:00:06,149
But the city has four thousand streets.

3
00:00:06,149 --> 00:00:07,874
Which one should Ravi pick?

4
00:00:08,424 --> 00:00:11,827
Last time, the rational model
said, check every option.

5
00:00:12,127 --> 00:00:14,790
But checking every street
would take a whole year.

6
00:00:15,090 --> 00:00:17,658
By then, the good shops
are already rented.

7
00:00:18,308 --> 00:00:19,834
Perfect takes too long.

8
00:00:19,834 --> 00:00:21,360
Ravi needs good enough.

9
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:24,226
So, what is bounded rationality?

10
00:00:24,526 --> 00:00:28,886
Bounded rationality means people want
to be rational, but their thinking

11
00:00:28,886 --> 00:00:30,557
has limits, or bounds.

12
00:00:31,157 --> 00:00:33,304
The idea comes from Herbert Simon.

13
00:00:33,654 --> 00:00:38,835
He wrote about these limits in his
1947 book, Administrative Behavior,

14
00:00:38,835 --> 00:00:42,219
and named the idea in
the nineteen fifties.

15
00:00:42,569 --> 00:00:46,869
In 1978, he won the
Nobel Prize in Economics.

16
00:00:47,519 --> 00:00:49,659
Every decision has three bounds.

17
00:00:50,059 --> 00:00:50,959
One.

18
00:00:50,608 --> 00:00:52,243
Incomplete information.

19
00:00:52,243 --> 00:00:54,449
Ravi cannot know every street.

20
00:00:54,799 --> 00:00:55,699
Two.

21
00:00:55,361 --> 00:00:56,485
Limited time.

22
00:00:56,485 --> 00:00:58,987
A good shop gets rented in days.

23
00:00:59,337 --> 00:01:00,237
Three.

24
00:01:00,019 --> 00:01:01,619
Limited brain power.

25
00:01:01,619 --> 00:01:04,594
Nobody can compare four thousand options.

26
00:01:05,244 --> 00:01:06,936
So what do people do?

27
00:01:06,936 --> 00:01:08,120
They satisfice.

28
00:01:08,470 --> 00:01:10,710
Satisfice is Simon's own word.

29
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:12,951
It joins satisfy and suffice.

30
00:01:13,301 --> 00:01:17,174
It means choosing the first option
that is good enough, not the perfect

31
00:01:17,174 --> 00:01:18,074
one.

32
00:01:18,265 --> 00:01:19,803
Watch how Ravi does it.

33
00:01:20,153 --> 00:01:22,027
First, he sets three must haves.

34
00:01:22,377 --> 00:01:24,385
Lots of people walking by.

35
00:01:24,385 --> 00:01:26,773
Rent under fifty thousand rupees.

36
00:01:26,773 --> 00:01:28,445
No chai stall nearby.

37
00:01:28,795 --> 00:01:31,794
Then he searches one small
area, street by street.

38
00:01:32,144 --> 00:01:34,919
Street one is busy, but
the rent is too high.

39
00:01:35,269 --> 00:01:38,698
Street two is cheap, but there
is a chai stall next door.

40
00:01:39,049 --> 00:01:41,379
Street three passes all three checks.

41
00:01:41,729 --> 00:01:43,867
Ravi stops, and signs that week.

42
00:01:43,867 --> 00:01:46,422
Three streets checked, not four thousand.

43
00:01:47,072 --> 00:01:48,871
Now compare the two models.

44
00:01:49,221 --> 00:01:51,193
The rational model optimises.

45
00:01:51,193 --> 00:01:54,843
It assumes you know every
option, with unlimited time.

46
00:01:55,193 --> 00:01:57,091
Bounded rationality satisfices.

47
00:01:57,091 --> 00:02:00,244
It accepts the limits,
and stops at good enough.

48
00:02:00,594 --> 00:02:03,483
Real managers satisfice most of the time.

49
00:02:03,483 --> 00:02:05,893
That is not lazy, it is smart.

50
00:02:06,543 --> 00:02:07,447
Quick check.

51
00:02:07,447 --> 00:02:11,806
Asha picks the first cup supplier
that is cheap, strong, and on time.

52
00:02:11,806 --> 00:02:13,101
What is she doing?

53
00:02:15,001 --> 00:02:15,901
Satisficing!

54
00:02:15,815 --> 00:02:20,095
She took the first option that met
her must haves, and stopped searching.

55
00:02:20,695 --> 00:02:21,705
So, remember.

56
00:02:22,005 --> 00:02:26,721
Bounded rationality means our decisions
are limited by information, time,

57
00:02:26,721 --> 00:02:28,010
and brain power.

58
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:31,862
So we satisfice, and pick the
first option that is good enough.

59
00:02:32,212 --> 00:02:37,873
The idea comes from Herbert Simon,
who won the Nobel Prize in 1978.

60
00:02:38,473 --> 00:02:42,967
Next time, Ravi learns to share his
work, with delegation of authority.
